{
  "$comment": "Approximate composition-date ranges per book, given for two broad scholarly traditions. Ranges, never points: the date a biblical book was written is genuinely contested, and presenting a single year would smuggle one school's position into an app whose premise is not doing that. The disagreement between the two columns is information, not noise. Years are negative for BCE. These are approximate consensus ranges compiled for orientation, not citations of a specific reference work -- see BACKLOG.md for the review item. For the deuterocanonical books the two traditions carry the same range: the dispute that separates them concerns the Hebrew canon, and inventing a disagreement here would be as misleading as hiding one elsewhere.",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "traditions": {
    "traditional": {
      "label": "Traditional dating",
      "description": "Follows traditional attributions of authorship -- Mosaic authorship of the Pentateuch, single-author Isaiah, sixth-century Daniel, and early dates for the Gospels and Pauline letters."
    },
    "critical": {
      "label": "Critical dating",
      "description": "Follows mainstream historical-critical scholarship -- composite Pentateuchal sources reaching final form in the Persian period, multiple authors in Isaiah, a Maccabean Daniel, and later dates for the Gospels and disputed letters."
    }
  },
